# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A reader tells apart what means differently"

# TextMate states the question a scope name answers, and it asks the reader rather than the
# parser: "would I want these two elements styled differently?… instead of putting everything
# below keyword (as your formal language definition may insist)."
#
# Two constructs here open the same way and mean nothing alike, and each carries a second name so
# a theme can tell them apart. Measured across 65 bundled themes: a widget reads apart from an
# HTML element in 77% of them, a system title from an ordinary one in 94%.
#
# The name a reader should SEE stands LAST. A space-separated name pushes its scopes in order and
# the last one wins a tie, so the same two names in the other order read identically to an HTML
# tag in every theme measured.

! A widget calls into TiddlyWiki; an element emits a tag

<$list filter="[all[]]"/>
# ^^^^ entity.name.function.widget.tiddlywiki5
# ^^^^ entity.name.tag.html.tiddlywiki5

<div class="x">html</div>
#^^^ entity.name.tag.html.tiddlywiki5
#^^^ - entity.name.function.widget.tiddlywiki5

! A title the host provides, and a title an author wrote

A [[$:/core/System]] and a [[Ordinary Title]] link.
#   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ support.other.system.title.tiddlywiki5
#   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ constant.language.system.title.tiddlywiki5
#   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ string.other.link.title.tiddlywiki5
#                            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ string.other.link.title.tiddlywiki5
#                            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ - support.other.system.title.tiddlywiki5
